  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConfidence continues to increase in accumulating snowfall over\nthe ridges Friday and Saturday. A low pressure center is \nexpected to cross into the Ohio Valley Wednesday night bringing \nsome showers to the region through the day on Thursday. \nProbabilities continue to show a trend of 40% to 60% of \nexceeding 0.50\". The frontal passage is expected to occur by 06Z\nFriday with much of the precip changing to snow showers with \nLake Enhanced snowfall expected. For most lowland areas, any\nprecipitation changing over to snow would occur with above\nfreezing surface temperatures and no accumulation especially\nduring the daylight hours.\n\nThe NBM as well as other ensembles, suggest some accumulating \nsnowfall over the WV ridges. In fact the probabilities show a \n90% potential of exceeding 3\". The potential of reaching winter\nstorm criteria is around 20% probability. Still, there are \nstill a couple factors to take into place concerning the \nconfidence. Here are the 2 scenarios possible:\n\n1. The expected flow aloft could make for a more progressive\nsystem and a quicker passage through the area. This would be due\nto a more southern passage and would result in little to no snow\nin the lower terrain and less than Advisory criteria for the WV\nmountains. \n\n2. A slower system passing through the the area a bit more north\nwould produce a scenario closer to the outcome described above.\nThis would bring advisory snow to the WV mountains and\npotentially the northern counties but the northern counties \nreaching advisory criteria is a 10% to 20% probability. At this\npoint with a more northerly low level flow Columbiana County has\nthe best chance of getting into a Lake Snow Band but\naccumulations above an inch are unlikely. The only other \nconcern would be the small potential of the WV Mountains \nreceiving warning criteria.",
